peerSolutions.content:
A direct link change works when the given node is not the head. Since this method receives only the node and not the list head, the code below also handles a head node by moving the old value into a new node after it. The visible order is still correct.

This changes the data stored in the supplied node, so it is useful when the judge checks list values rather than node identity.

```java
class Solution {
    public void insertBeforeGivenNode(ListNode node, int X) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        int oldValue = node.data;
        ListNode oldNext = node.next;

        node.data = X;

        ListNode movedNode = new ListNode(oldValue);
        movedNode.prev = node;
        movedNode.next = oldNext;
        node.next = movedNode;

        if (oldNext != null) {
            oldNext.prev = movedNode;
        }
    }
}
```

The empty-node case is ignored safely. A one-node list simply becomes two nodes in the order `X, oldValue`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The target already knows both sides of its position through `prev` and `next`. The new node is placed between `previous` and `node`, then both neighboring links are fixed.

Dry run with `7 <-> 11` and `X = 3`: the new node gets `prev = 7` and `next = 11`. The final links are `7 <-> 3 <-> 11`. For an empty target reference, the function does nothing.

```javascript
class Solution {
  insertBeforeGivenNode(node, X) {
    if (node === null || node === undefined) {
      return;
    }

    const previous = node.prev;
    const inserted = new ListNode(X, previous, node);

    node.prev = inserted;

    if (previous !== null && previous !== undefined) {
      previous.next = inserted;
    }
  }
}
```

The input list is changed directly. The new node is the only allocation, and no traversal is needed because the given node already provides the required position.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, excluding the inserted node.

The new node has to go between `node.prev` and `node`. I save the previous node first, then update all four affected links. If the given node is the first node, there is no previous link to change, but the method cannot change an external head pointer.

For example, with `4 <-> 8` and `node = 8`, inserting `6` changes the links to `4 <-> 6 <-> 8`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void insertBeforeGivenNode(ListNode* node, int X) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        ListNode* previous = node->prev;
        ListNode* inserted = new ListNode(X);

        inserted->prev = previous;
        inserted->next = node;
        node->prev = inserted;

        if (previous != nullptr) {
            previous->next = inserted;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the new node.

I kept the operation local. First, the new node points left to the old previous node and right to the supplied node. Then the old neighbors are connected back to the new node.

The `previous == null` check matters for a node at the front. In that case, there is no left neighbor whose `next` field can be changed.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public void insertBeforeGivenNode(ListNode node, int X) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        ListNode previous = node.prev;
        ListNode inserted = new ListNode(X);

        inserted.prev = previous;
        inserted.next = node;
        node.prev = inserted;

        if (previous != null) {
            previous.next = inserted;
        }
    }
}
```

This does not copy or rebuild the list. It changes the existing links and allocates just one node. As with any method that receives no head reference, inserting before the current head cannot update the caller's head variable.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.
